WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:02.570
Year is known in advance,

2
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:07.170
so omitting it is a modelling decision
rather than a leakage restriction.

3
00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:10.870
Every training observation
belongs to twenty eleven,

4
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.430
where the raw year flag is zero.

5
00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:18.350
A linear weight gradient equals
residual times feature value.

6
00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:23.250
Multiplying by that zero feature
gives zero for every training example.

7
00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:28.690
The data therefore cannot identify a
year to year effect through this column.

8
00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:33.710
A year flag of one in twenty twelve
would use an unsupported coefficient.

9
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.130
We leave it out of the first schema.

10
00:00:38.109 --> 00:00:42.749
We retain month and omit the
coarser season code from this first

11
00:00:42.749 --> 00:00:43.819
representation.

12
00:00:44.549 --> 00:00:48.239
The source documents disagree
about some season names,

13
00:00:48.409 --> 00:00:52.159
so we retain the codes without
assigning uncertain names.

14
00:00:52.649 --> 00:00:56.799
We have not tested whether including
season would improve a predictor.

15
00:00:57.589 --> 00:01:02.029
A later feature comparison can ask that
question using the same frozen validation

16
00:01:02.029 --> 00:01:02.899
period.

17
00:01:03.969 --> 00:01:08.779
Source documentation also differs on
some weather normalization details.

18
00:01:09.329 --> 00:01:13.909
We validate retained numeric ranges
without inventing disputed temperature

19
00:01:13.909 --> 00:01:14.799
conversions.

20
00:01:17.025 --> 00:01:21.195
Exactly one hour indicator
is active for every row.

21
00:01:21.745 --> 00:01:26.215
Add two to every hour coefficient,
and subtract two from the bias.

22
00:01:26.825 --> 00:01:32.175
The prediction stays unchanged, because
one added two cancels the subtracted two.

23
00:01:32.785 --> 00:01:36.975
Full category groups and the intercept
therefore contain dependencies.

24
00:01:37.845 --> 00:01:40.635
Some flags overlap with
calendar groups as well.

25
00:01:41.145 --> 00:01:45.965
A model can have useful predictions while
individual coefficient values are not

26
00:01:45.965 --> 00:01:46.655
unique.

27
00:01:47.245 --> 00:01:52.065
We should be careful about treating one
coefficient as a unique effect or a causal

28
00:01:52.065 --> 00:01:53.015
explanation.

29
00:01:55.067 --> 00:02:00.607
Dropping one reference indicator from each
of the three category groups removes three

30
00:02:00.607 --> 00:02:01.277
columns.

31
00:02:02.027 --> 00:02:04.037
Forty five becomes forty two,

32
00:02:04.447 --> 00:02:05.997
with the two flags retained.

33
00:02:07.127 --> 00:02:11.487
Coefficients then describe differences
relative to the omitted categories for

34
00:02:11.487 --> 00:02:12.257
those groups.

35
00:02:13.247 --> 00:02:15.577
Other flag dependencies can still remain.

36
00:02:16.607 --> 00:02:19.317
Regularization is another
later modelling choice.

37
00:02:20.027 --> 00:02:22.517
Neither change has been
silently added here.

38
00:02:22.967 --> 00:02:27.297
Our first schema keeps all categories
visible, records the dependencies

39
00:02:27.747 --> 00:02:30.917
and leaves later comparisons
to measure their consequences.

40
00:02:33.067 --> 00:02:37.457
The hour domain, zero through twenty
three, comes from the calendar.

41
00:02:38.187 --> 00:02:40.697
We do not discover it by
reading future observations.

42
00:02:41.567 --> 00:02:44.317
This fixed encoder has
no fitted statistics.

43
00:02:45.107 --> 00:02:49.137
Its outputs are already zero or one,
and no scaler is fitted.

44
00:02:49.687 --> 00:02:53.797
If we later learn a mean,
scale or selected feature set,

45
00:02:54.127 --> 00:02:56.397
that learning belongs to training only.

46
00:02:57.167 --> 00:02:59.537
Validation applies the saved transform.

47
00:03:00.187 --> 00:03:04.357
A chronological cross validation
fold needs its own training fit,

48
00:03:04.447 --> 00:03:07.337
following the same
boundary inside every fold.

49
00:03:09.295 --> 00:03:13.505
A valid calendar category can be
missing from a small training sample.

50
00:03:13.955 --> 00:03:15.725
Its schema position still exists,

51
00:03:15.915 --> 00:03:19.125
although the model has no
supporting examples for its effect.

52
00:03:19.995 --> 00:03:24.545
An impossible hour, twenty four,
is different and must fail validation.

53
00:03:25.515 --> 00:03:31.465
Missing fields, extra fields such as the
target, and noninteger codes also fail.

54
00:03:32.335 --> 00:03:35.685
The working day flag must
agree with weekday and holiday.

55
00:03:36.515 --> 00:03:39.185
Training only feature
counts describe coverage.

56
00:03:40.095 --> 00:03:41.645
Constant columns keep their positions,

57
00:03:41.935 --> 00:03:44.785
and we never divide by a
zero standard deviation.

58
00:03:47.165 --> 00:03:50.695
Circular coordinates make a
clock relationship numerical.

59
00:03:51.425 --> 00:03:55.255
At hour zero,
sine and cosine are zero and one.

60
00:03:55.685 --> 00:03:56.755
At hour twenty three,

61
00:03:57.325 --> 00:04:02.595
they are approximately minus zero point
two five eight eight and zero point nine

62
00:04:02.595 --> 00:04:03.735
six five nine.

63
00:04:04.325 --> 00:04:07.975
Their Euclidean distance is
about zero point two six one one,

64
00:04:08.185 --> 00:04:10.915
while raw hour numbers
differ by twenty three.

65
00:04:11.585 --> 00:04:14.035
That encodes closeness across midnight.

66
00:04:14.865 --> 00:04:19.845
A linear predictor over one sine and
cosine pair still has a smooth single

67
00:04:19.845 --> 00:04:21.095
harmonic shape,

68
00:04:21.365 --> 00:04:24.995
which is a modelling assumption rather
than evidence of better forecasts.

69
00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:32.370
Full one hot encoding with an
intercept has another subtle property.

70
00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:36.130
Every row activates
exactly one hour indicator.

71
00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:41.180
Add five to all twenty four hour
coefficients and subtract five from the

72
00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:41.730
intercept.

73
00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:46.450
The active hour contributes five
more and the intercept five less,

74
00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:48.990
so every prediction stays the same.

75
00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:52.470
Ten plus twenty becomes
five plus twenty five.

76
00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:56.350
Both totals are thirty before
the other shared terms.

77
00:04:57.080 --> 00:05:02.200
A coefficient can therefore lack a unique
isolated interpretation even while the

78
00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:03.750
prediction is well defined.

79
00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:09.790
Does that dependence mean the dataset
cannot be used for prediction?

80
00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:14.830
Pause and distinguish predictions
from parameter interpretation.

81
00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:19.530
Equivalent coefficient sets can
produce the same prediction function.

82
00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:25.360
Dropping a reference category or applying
regularization can select a representation

83
00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:26.630
or solution,

84
00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:29.690
but those are explicit later choices.

85
00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:35.190
Do not call an isolated hourly
weight a unique causal effect.

86
00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:40.880
Keep the schema fixed and evaluate choices
with the same observations before claiming

87
00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:42.210
that one is better.
